WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.860
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.381
You've clearly had a very impressive
career but before becoming one of the-

00:00:09.381 --> 00:00:11.032
&gt;&gt; That's not what my mother says-

00:00:11.032 --> 00:00:11.736
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:11.736 --> 00:00:12.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.375
&gt;&gt; Before becoming one of the top venture

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:19.730
capitalists in the world, being knighted
by the queen, you were a boy from Wales.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:23.100
What did you want to be when you grew up?

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.960
&gt;&gt; Actually, not that this game or
sport is played in America, and so

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.740
it won't translate, I wanted to
be a cricket player, a cricketer.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:36.560
But I was a coward, and
the ball was very, very hard.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.540
And so I spent most of my time in a sport,
whether it was cricket or rugby, trying to

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.538
avoid being anywhere near the ball, so-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:46.538 --> 00:00:50.440
&gt;&gt; I have a friend actually who became

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.470
a spectacular rugby player,
he played for Wales which, and

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.920
I told him that the only
thing that separated him and

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.540
me on the rugby field was
when he took to the field.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.772
He wanted the ball to
come in his direction.

00:01:06.772 --> 00:01:11.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I of course wanted the reverse,

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:13.620
I never wanted to see it
come in my direction.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.610
As I got a bit older I became

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:24.260
interested in journalism and
when I was at Oxford,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.720
and I knew that I wanted to be
a journalist when I was at Oxford.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.738
&gt;&gt; So I'm interested in the story
of how your family came to Wales.

00:01:34.738 --> 00:01:40.480
It was quite inspiring that your
parents actually escaped Germany

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.985
during the second World War, arrived in
Wales as refugees, in the UK as refugees.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.320
How did their struggles and
their experience

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.140
shape you early on in sort of what
you wanted to do with your life?

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.720
&gt;&gt; Well they were very lucky.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.550
They didn't escape during
the second World War.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.520
They escaped beforehand.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.362
Not many people escaped Germany
during the second World War.

00:02:03.362 --> 00:02:08.497
And my father left Germany,
he was born in Munich and

00:02:08.497 --> 00:02:12.740
went to Britain when he was a 14 year old.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.852
My mother went on something called
the Kinder transport which was

00:02:17.852 --> 00:02:22.900
10,000 kids from Germany
basically that were rescued and

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:28.050
put in foster homes and

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:32.700
they then met subsequently in Britain and

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.890
after the war they got married.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:44.040
And those searing experiences, difficult.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.620
I'm sure there are people
here either who have

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:53.360
first hand familial experience
of something as disquieting,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.280
unsettling and wrenching as
those sorts of experiences, but

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:04.100
those are the experiences that
come down through generations.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:09.250
My father's father served in
the German army during World War I.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.300
He was decorated.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.310
And my father's mother was
a nurse in the German army.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.370
During World War I and then both of them

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.780
were killed,
they were murdered by the Nazis.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.856
And my father died about 15, 16 years ago.

00:03:31.856 --> 00:03:37.107
My mother [LAUGH] I saw her last week,
is 95 and

00:03:37.107 --> 00:03:44.810
is still vibrant but those experiences,
they color everything.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:49.370
They hang over everything
like a dark cloud.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.250
It is always the anxiety and

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:58.170
the fear of the whole world being
ripped out from underneath you and

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:02.580
it colors the way that you live,
understandably.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.343
&gt;&gt; And that drives you today, still?

00:04:05.343 --> 00:04:06.787
&gt;&gt; Well I don't know about that.

00:04:06.787 --> 00:04:11.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But it certainly had a profound effect

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:16.851
on my parents and
they came to Wales as outsiders and

00:04:16.851 --> 00:04:20.484
obviously Britain I owe everything,

00:04:20.484 --> 00:04:25.252
my parents owe everything
to the fact that Britain

00:04:25.252 --> 00:04:31.130
opened its borders and
allowed them to settle there.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.730
But it was not easy.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.020
They were in a minority.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:37.870
They weren't exactly, I mean,

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:43.840
I was one of I think three Jewish kids
at the high school that I went to.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.190
So I've always, one always felt,

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:54.460
an outsider in the delineation
between religions in Britain which

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:59.090
as 40, 50 years ago was very, very sharp.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:03.900
And so if you didn't belong to the
majority it was very obvious that you were

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.320
on the outside, so
that's obviously had a huge effect.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:09.450
&gt;&gt; Well, you did them very proud.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:12.290
You worked really hard to get into Oxford.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:16.680
And I'm sure you had many opportunities
professionally in Europe, but

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.390
you decided to come to the United States.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:20.790
Why the United States?

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:24.430
&gt;&gt; Well, I didn't have many professional
opportunities in Europe because I grew up

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:29.190
in the Britain that
predated Margaret Thatcher.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:37.520
And those were extremely
difficult times in Britain,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.520
I mean obviously Britain is going through
its own issues today with Brexit.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.190
But the times in the 1970s
people who weren't around.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:48.280
There's no reason that anyone
here should know about it.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:55.060
But it was a strike-ridden country,
where you never knew whether

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:59.000
the electricity was going to work, whether
there was going to be a gas strike,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.410
whether the railways wouldn't run, or
whether the newspapers would be delivered,

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:08.530
because there'd be a wildcat strike,
and I wanted to be a journalist.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.160
And I wanted to work on Fleet Street.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.140
And I wanted to work at the Times or
the Daily Telegraph or the Guardian or

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:17.140
one of the big broad sheet newspapers that

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:20.390
at that point dominated
journalism in Britain.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:26.920
But, because of union restrictions
the Fleet Street newspapers,

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:32.040
the big newspapers, were not permitted to
hire anybody straight out of under grad.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:32.770
You had to go and

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:38.420
serve a multiyear apprenticeship
on small provincial newspapers.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:42.410
And I had no appetite in doing that.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.430
I wanted to work on Fleet Street.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.454
I want it to sort of work in
the premiere league, so to speak.

00:06:47.454 --> 00:06:50.860
And I had a,

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.150
I went to see a gentleman who was
the editor of the Daily Telegraph,

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:59.080
a fellow who,
he was a Fleet Street legend and

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:03.950
a character on whom Evelyn Waugh
had based the novel Scoop.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.970
Which is hilarious if
you've never read it.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:12.540
And he said if I was your age,
get out of Britain.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:13.125
Go to America.

00:07:13.125 --> 00:07:19.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And that was what sent me to America.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:24.290
It was that sort of weird
peculiar encounter.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:25.790
And I often think about that.

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:28.660
That's what made me up sticks and leave.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.500
I mean, well, and I was lucky.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.190
I wouldn't have been able to come to
America had I not got a scholarship

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.380
because I didn't have the money and
all the rest of it.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:43.294
But I got lucky in that,
and I came to America,

00:07:43.294 --> 00:07:48.208
this is 1976, not knowing where anything

00:07:48.208 --> 00:07:52.380
would lead but have obviously stayed.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, 40 years you said.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.270
So you started out when you
got here as a journalist for

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:58.320
Time Magazine-
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:02.190
&gt;&gt; In San Francisco Actually,
not in San Francisco.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.560
In Detroit, of all places.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:04.310
&gt;&gt; In Detroit?

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:07.630
&gt;&gt; Covering the first collapse of
the Chrysler Automobile Company.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:12.300
&gt;&gt; And then you made your way to
San Francisco to cover technology.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.090
And during this time you
got to know Steve Jobs.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.375
What do you believe made Jobs
such a unique innovator and

00:08:19.375 --> 00:08:21.869
leader in the time that
you spent with him?

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.040
&gt;&gt; So this is, again,
this a long time ago.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.337
This is 1980.

00:08:29.337 --> 00:08:36.073
And Microsoft was a company that I
remember going to an event at Apple,

00:08:36.073 --> 00:08:42.604
one of these sort of management events,
small management events.

00:08:42.604 --> 00:08:45.489
Probably this was probably 1982 or '83.

00:08:45.489 --> 00:08:51.490
And Steve had a sort of
fireside chat like this.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:53.540
And he asked the guest,

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:57.570
a fellow who at that point was a Morgan
Stanley analyst called Ben Roselyn,

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:01.620
who later went on to found a very
successful venture capital firm.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:06.680
He said, so Ben,
pick the year when Microsoft,

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:11.785
you think Microsoft will break the $10
million revenue barrier, right?

00:09:11.785 --> 00:09:15.039
$10 million, and
Ben was scratching his head.

00:09:15.039 --> 00:09:17.578
So it's a very, very long time ago.

00:09:17.578 --> 00:09:19.638
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:09:19.638 --> 00:09:23.763
that was the only point I
was lamely trying to make.

00:09:29.155 --> 00:09:33.105
I realized I hadn't met anyone like Steve.

00:09:33.105 --> 00:09:37.745
I'd spend time with fellow called
Lee Iaccoca who was a living legend

00:09:37.745 --> 00:09:41.745
in American industry at that point,
and been the president

00:09:41.745 --> 00:09:45.916
to the Ford Motor company and
then became the CEO of Chrysler.

00:09:45.916 --> 00:09:53.032
And Steve was the first person who's
salesmanship was on a par with Iaccoca's.

00:09:53.032 --> 00:09:57.060
Iaccoca was just mesmerizing character,
but

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:02.420
he didn't have the product and
engineering attributes that Steve had.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:09.983
And obviously, there was a difference
in age of probably 35 years or so.

00:10:09.983 --> 00:10:16.213
But Steve was a spellbinder.

00:10:16.213 --> 00:10:23.556
And obviously,
as everybody knows these days,

00:10:23.556 --> 00:10:28.830
a very difficult and
complicated character.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:31.350
But he was an extraordinary, and

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:37.312
even though I had all sorts of issues
in dealing with him, have nothing but

00:10:37.312 --> 00:10:42.820
massive admiration for

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:47.471
what it is that he eventually went on and
did.

00:10:47.471 --> 00:10:51.095
And but as I said, it's a long time ago.

00:10:51.095 --> 00:10:56.478
And I think at that time I
probably held a minority

00:10:56.478 --> 00:11:02.009
opinion of how spectacular
an individual he was.

00:11:02.009 --> 00:11:05.411
Because later, he was,
again, ancient history,

00:11:05.411 --> 00:11:08.441
he was fired from
the company that he founded.

00:11:08.441 --> 00:11:11.027
Which to me always seemed
like a real travesty.

00:11:12.494 --> 00:11:16.755
&gt;&gt; So following your time with
Time Magazine, you would make the switch

00:11:16.755 --> 00:11:21.610
to the venture capital industry by
joining Sequoia, which is very uncommon.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:24.240
How did that happen?

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:28.680
&gt;&gt; Well I'd left time because,
while I've enjoyed four years or so

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.130
as a journalist, I also got frustrated.

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:34.390
It was a big company,
I was working on the periphery of it.

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:39.388
And sort of for some reason it always
felt I didn't want to be a journalist

00:11:39.388 --> 00:11:41.234
when I was 30 years old.

00:11:41.234 --> 00:11:43.532
And I had that as some
sort of mental benchmark.

00:11:43.532 --> 00:11:45.370
Goodness knows where that came from.

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:49.460
I think it was largely because, again,
back then, I saw what happened to people.

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:50.550
It was eye opening to me,

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:55.410
I saw what happened to people who stayed
in journalism and were 60 years old.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:59.750
And certainly, if they worked for Time,
which was a sort of cushy job, but

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:04.865
you got sent all over the world and
you wound up as an alcoholic.

00:12:04.865 --> 00:12:09.824
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which appeared to me

00:12:09.824 --> 00:12:12.925
to be a really beckoning
career trajectory.

00:12:12.925 --> 00:12:17.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so I left.

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:21.850
And together with somebody else,
I started a little company that published

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:26.320
newsletters, stage conferences
about the technology industry.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.290
And so much for my.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:34.986
And that fellow stuck with it,
and eventually many years later,

00:12:34.986 --> 00:12:39.988
no thanks to me,
Dow Jones bought that company.

00:12:39.988 --> 00:12:42.894
But I always felt that it was
going to be a small company.

00:12:42.894 --> 00:12:48.014
And I heard through my work at Time,
through my work at this little company,

00:12:48.014 --> 00:12:51.856
I'd met a lot of people around
the technology industry.

00:12:51.856 --> 00:12:56.933
And had met a lot of people in
the venture capital business.

00:12:56.933 --> 00:13:02.588
Time had run a cover story that I
had reported on about the venture

00:13:02.588 --> 00:13:07.930
industry that had Arthur Rock
who was the original founder,

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:12.454
investor in Intel, and
then of Apple on the cover.

00:13:12.454 --> 00:13:16.898
And during the process of that, had met
a lot of people in the venture business.

00:13:16.898 --> 00:13:21.100
And when I came to California,
I didn't know about Silicon Valley,

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:26.118
didn't know anything about the venture
industry, and had got interested in it.

00:13:26.118 --> 00:13:30.921
And made a list of five firms where I

00:13:30.921 --> 00:13:35.400
knew somebody, wrote to them.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:39.690
You wrote to them in those days,
you didn't e-mail them.

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:44.074
And got interviewed at all,

00:13:44.074 --> 00:13:48.644
got rejected at four of them.

00:13:48.644 --> 00:13:52.992
Journalist, history major,
no experience working in

00:13:52.992 --> 00:13:58.170
a Silicon Valley company,
therefore totally useless.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:01.641
Probably correct on most of those counts.

00:14:01.641 --> 00:14:06.892
And then Don Valentine, who was
the founder of Sequoia, decided that

00:14:06.892 --> 00:14:12.625
he needed to do something to change
Sequoia's image with minority hiring.

00:14:12.625 --> 00:14:18.837
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:18.837 --> 00:14:21.833
&gt;&gt; And so, decide to hire a history major.

00:14:21.833 --> 00:14:23.702
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:23.702 --> 00:14:25.944
&gt;&gt; And but it was head scratcher for

00:14:25.944 --> 00:14:30.764
most of Don's peers in the venture
business wondering why on Earth he

00:14:30.764 --> 00:14:33.855
was wasting his time
with somebody like me.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:38.018
And so that's how it happened.

00:14:38.018 --> 00:14:39.540
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was great good fortune.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:43.813
If Don had said, voted like the others,

00:14:43.813 --> 00:14:47.340
I don't know what I'd be doing.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:52.540
&gt;&gt; So in the era of the prized
engineering degree in Silicon Valley,

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:56.426
what value does that broad based
humanities education bring?

00:14:56.426 --> 00:15:00.640
Given that about 50% of
last year's incoming class

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:03.550
came in with a Humanities undergraduate
degree, what value does that bring to

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.120
the technology ecosystem
here in Silicon Valley?

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:07.170
What do you think?

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:13.730
&gt;&gt; Well,
I obviously have a prejudice and bias.

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:21.050
And knowing full well
that I wish I understood

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:24.570
more about the details of technology and
engineering and all the rest of it.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:29.950
But if you're in the investing business
in Silicon Valley, you cover such a wide

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:34.520
waterfront of investing,
that it's very difficult.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:39.480
You can't be, no matter how numerate and
technical you are,

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.050
you cannot be an expert in everything.

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:47.080
So the ability to be able to, and

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:52.420
this is much like being a journalist,
to start on

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:57.510
an endeavor where you know nothing,
where you gather a lot of materials and

00:15:57.510 --> 00:16:02.110
facts, where you have to
distill all of those facts.

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:08.115
And then form a cogent opinion and
make a decision.

00:16:08.115 --> 00:16:13.572
It's not unlike writing a story,
writing a history essay,

00:16:13.572 --> 00:16:19.570
or making an investment, or
helping to determine an investment.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:23.297
What comes after that,
after making the investment,

00:16:23.297 --> 00:16:28.319
all the sorts of things that these days
go towards helping a little company

00:16:28.319 --> 00:16:33.345
that might be composed of three people
becoming something significant,

00:16:33.345 --> 00:16:39.285
that's very different set of skills which,
I think, just experience brings along.

00:16:39.285 --> 00:16:43.631
It doesn't matter whether or not you
have a technical degree or anything.

00:16:43.631 --> 00:16:48.608
And then, the ability to be
a story-teller and a clear communicator.

00:16:48.608 --> 00:16:52.841
I think those are really,
very, very helpful pursuits.

00:16:52.841 --> 00:16:53.398
But again,

00:16:53.398 --> 00:16:56.807
as I said, I have a bias and prejudice,
and I was looking at the numbers.

00:16:56.807 --> 00:16:59.795
At Stanford, for example you had,

00:16:59.795 --> 00:17:05.173
if I've got the numbers right,
which they're roughly right,

00:17:05.173 --> 00:17:10.561
you had 647 undergrads in
1965 who were history majors.

00:17:10.561 --> 00:17:16.106
In 2014, which was the last set
of data that I saw, you had 84.

00:17:16.106 --> 00:17:18.234
So they're a threatened
species around here.

00:17:18.234 --> 00:17:19.827
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well,

00:17:19.827 --> 00:17:23.335
I think you put those skills
to good use at Sequoia,

00:17:23.335 --> 00:17:28.732
given that it's consistently listed as
one of the top venture capital firms.

00:17:28.732 --> 00:17:30.547
And I have to get this statistic right,

00:17:30.547 --> 00:17:32.864
because it kind of blew my
mind when I first saw it.

00:17:32.864 --> 00:17:38.117
Since 1972,
Sequoia has backed companies that now have

00:17:38.117 --> 00:17:43.484
an aggregate public market
value of over $3.3 trillion.

00:17:43.484 --> 00:17:48.925
To put that in relative size, that's
larger than the current GDP of the UK.

00:17:48.925 --> 00:17:51.685
What are the most-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:51.685 --> 00:17:52.269
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:17:52.269 --> 00:17:57.548
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So-

00:17:57.548 --> 00:17:58.301
&gt;&gt; Put down!

00:17:58.301 --> 00:18:03.401
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So what do you think
are the most important leadership

00:18:03.401 --> 00:18:09.018
traits needed to build enduring
organizations such as Sequoia?

00:18:09.018 --> 00:18:10.282
&gt;&gt; Well, that's nice of you to say.

00:18:14.026 --> 00:18:21.077
Obviously, that's a question that's at
the heart of our pursuit at Sequoia,

00:18:21.077 --> 00:18:26.024
and also the heart of the pursuit
of the very distinctive

00:18:26.024 --> 00:18:30.671
companies that we would like
to be an investor with.

00:18:30.671 --> 00:18:33.511
And being able to think and

00:18:33.511 --> 00:18:39.069
work in 20 year increments
is such an advantage.

00:18:39.069 --> 00:18:42.680
And I know that sounds a little weird and

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:47.605
a little peculiar, and
obviously you can talk a grand

00:18:47.605 --> 00:18:52.986
fashion about a 20 year plan and
it all sounds very good.

00:18:52.986 --> 00:18:58.229
But then, you also have to work
on what this afternoon brings.

00:18:58.229 --> 00:19:02.742
But having a sense of eventually
where your compass is heading and

00:19:02.742 --> 00:19:06.515
where it's set, and
the trajectory you want to go on,

00:19:06.515 --> 00:19:09.976
and then breaking it down
into all sorts of steps.

00:19:09.976 --> 00:19:15.903
Knowing that every step along the journey
is going to require different people,

00:19:15.903 --> 00:19:19.587
different skills, different relationships,

00:19:19.587 --> 00:19:25.537
different virtues that you constantly
going to have to change along the course.

00:19:25.537 --> 00:19:30.523
And reacting to changing circumstances,
changing market conditions, and

00:19:30.523 --> 00:19:35.890
all the rest of it, but never losing
sight of eventually where you want to go.

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:41.740
I think that's the biggest thing
that has helped with us and helped

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:46.360
with some of these companies that we've
had the good fortune to be involved with.

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:49.250
Now, obviously not everything
works out like that.

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:53.089
A lot of companies start,
and maybe they flourish for

00:19:53.089 --> 00:19:56.768
a certain period of time,
and then they're sold.

00:19:56.768 --> 00:20:02.377
But the ones that we're looking for,
I mean, now where are we, 2019?

00:20:02.377 --> 00:20:07.516
It's 20 years since we invested in Google,

00:20:07.516 --> 00:20:13.460
right, I still own the majority
of my Google shares.

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:17.052
And when somebody asks me today,

00:20:17.052 --> 00:20:22.964
why is a client interested
investing in a our company?

00:20:22.964 --> 00:20:27.817
I'll always say to them, if I think it
happens to be true in that particular

00:20:27.817 --> 00:20:31.452
regard, because some companies
don't fit in to that.

00:20:31.452 --> 00:20:37.234
It's because if we're all fortunate,
if the sun shines on us,

00:20:37.234 --> 00:20:42.598
we can be shareholders in your
company 20 years from now.

00:20:42.598 --> 00:20:47.475
Today, Airbnb,
I think we invested in Airbnb $600,000 in

00:20:47.475 --> 00:20:50.135
2008 I think, maybe 2009.

00:20:50.135 --> 00:20:57.307
It's ten years, happens like that,
and the company is still private.

00:20:57.307 --> 00:21:02.175
Stripe, which is a payment services
company we invested in when it was

00:21:02.175 --> 00:21:05.154
three or four people, that was 2010.

00:21:05.154 --> 00:21:11.270
Again, nine years later, these are very,
very long journeys that we're on.

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:17.067
And it takes time and patience,
and spectacular people,

00:21:17.067 --> 00:21:20.617
and a massive market opportunity,

00:21:20.617 --> 00:21:24.876
to build the real
companies that matter and

00:21:24.876 --> 00:21:29.627
have an influence and
great impact on society.

00:21:29.627 --> 00:21:33.204
&gt;&gt; And you and Sir Alex talk about
the importance of identifying high

00:21:33.204 --> 00:21:36.595
potential talent in building
these types of organizations.

00:21:36.595 --> 00:21:40.752
So the example he uses is a footballer
some of you may know by the name

00:21:40.752 --> 00:21:43.215
of David Beckham, who is pretty good.

00:21:43.215 --> 00:21:47.022
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What characteristics are you looking

00:21:47.022 --> 00:21:50.419
for when trying to identify
this high potential talent,

00:21:50.419 --> 00:21:53.758
whether it be a founder or
somebody you hire at Sequoia?

00:21:57.083 --> 00:22:00.958
&gt;&gt; The reason that I approached
Alex to write the book about

00:22:00.958 --> 00:22:05.907
Manchester United was, for
whatever perverse reason people have been

00:22:05.907 --> 00:22:09.980
asking me to write a book about
Silicon Valley or Sequoia.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:11.860
And I had no interest in doing that, but

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:18.120
I was interested in writing
about enduring organizations.

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:23.060
And I picked United because it was
a long way away from Silicon Valley, but

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:28.800
because I knew that he shared
the approach that we cherished as well.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.090
One of which, Patrick, was the one
that you just fingered, which was,

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:39.000
identifying people when they are young and
then helping them develop.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.999
And why?

00:22:40.999 --> 00:22:47.428
Part of it is because you're
not taking a lot of risk.

00:22:47.428 --> 00:22:48.771
Beckham, I think,

00:22:48.771 --> 00:22:54.780
they knew about Beckham when Beckham was
eight years old at Manchester United.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:58.235
Now, we're not going around Stanford
looking for eight year olds, but-

00:22:58.235 --> 00:23:00.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:03.932
&gt;&gt; We do like hiring at Sequoia people who

00:23:03.932 --> 00:23:04.987
are young.

00:23:04.987 --> 00:23:08.964
Because it's a business that,
even though it looks simple, and

00:23:08.964 --> 00:23:13.232
today I understand everybody is
an investor, from someone who writes

00:23:13.232 --> 00:23:17.710
$5,000 checks to somebody who
writes a much bigger kind of check.

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:22.620
But to do it well in the fashion
that we like to do it, takes time,

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:28.470
and patience, and persistence,
and a willingness to learn.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:32.800
And assuming people like that flourish,

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:37.630
you have incredible loyalty, and
they'll stay with you forever.

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:44.335
And so that was true for a lot of
the players who played for Sir Alex.

00:23:44.335 --> 00:23:47.624
And I think being able to
grow organically like that,

00:23:47.624 --> 00:23:52.450
it's such an enormous competitive
advantage, where you have a stable team of

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:56.640
people which you're always amending,
you're always changing.

00:23:56.640 --> 00:24:01.150
Someone is perhaps no longer playing
as well as they used to play, so

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:04.240
you figure out a way to Deal with that.

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:07.733
But then you're always
replenishing the bench.

00:24:07.733 --> 00:24:12.380
But you're doing it in an imperceptible
fashion to people on the outside.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:17.670
So that all they see is, in the soccer
metaphor, the winning team on the field,

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:20.290
without realizing that actually there

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:24.530
are two players on there today who
weren't on that team 24 months ago.

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:28.690
But that's worked spectacularly for us.

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:33.826
We've got these great who's

00:24:33.826 --> 00:24:38.290
a graduate at GSB and
he became, he want to PayPal.

00:24:38.290 --> 00:24:40.907
And after PayPal he joined Sequoia.

00:24:40.907 --> 00:24:45.165
And that was probably now 17,
18 years ago?

00:24:45.165 --> 00:24:49.454
And he's just spectacular,
he's just one example.

00:24:49.454 --> 00:24:52.330
We got a whole bunch of other
people like that, so it's great.

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:55.340
&gt;&gt; One of the characteristics
you mentioned that I was

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:58.970
really intrigued by is
this idea of obsession.

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:02.520
So the best founders, the best athletes,

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:06.680
tend to be obsessed with whatever it is,
the thing that they're working on.

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:12.860
I was curious, what do you think obsession
means to you, and how does it feel?

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:14.907
How do you know it when you see it?

00:25:16.646 --> 00:25:20.721
&gt;&gt; There's a gentleman called
Apoorva Mehta, who's the founder and

00:25:20.721 --> 00:25:23.840
CEO of Instacart,
the online grocery company.

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:30.490
And we've been with Apoorva on his journey
for a considerable period of time now.

00:25:30.490 --> 00:25:35.250
And before we invested,
I asked him how he had

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:39.430
happened on grocery delivery as
a business that he wanted to be in.

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:42.240
And he said he'd played around with and

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:46.190
toyed with a variety of other
businesses that he wanted to start, and

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:51.150
he'd got down the road on, I forget,
two, three, four of those.

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:56.118
But he said the only one, or
he realized Instacart was the one for

00:25:56.118 --> 00:26:00.810
him when it was the last,
the idea of the business was the last

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:04.766
thing that he thought about
when he went to sleep and

00:26:04.766 --> 00:26:10.011
the first thing that he thought about
when he woke up in the morning.

00:26:10.011 --> 00:26:16.060
And to me, that was as good a definition
of obsession as any I've heard.

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:21.832
But it's that sort of
full-on experience that

00:26:21.832 --> 00:26:26.920
you can never stop thinking about and
you don't switch off.

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:31.100
I'll give you another
example from yesteryear,

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:35.490
which was, again,
this is back to the early 1980s.

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:41.090
And I had been up in Seattle working
on a story about Microsoft for Time.

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:45.312
And again, this was when
Microsoft was still private and

00:26:45.312 --> 00:26:46.960
it was still quite small.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.900
And Bill Gates gave me a ride
to the airport in his car.

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:57.345
And not many people can say that
they were chauffeured by Bill Gates.

00:26:57.345 --> 00:26:59.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In fact,

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:02.125
I may put that on my LinkedIn profile.

00:27:02.125 --> 00:27:08.665
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Above all other forms of education.

00:27:08.665 --> 00:27:13.053
And the radio was missing in the car.

00:27:13.053 --> 00:27:14.840
Big, gaping hole in the dashboard.

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:16.610
And I said, Bill, so what happened to you?

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:17.723
Did you get ripped off?

00:27:19.903 --> 00:27:21.420
And he said no, I had it taken out.

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:24.690
Why'd you have it taken out?

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:31.960
Well, I drive from my home to the office,
which is 7 minutes and 32 seconds.

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:36.620
And then, I'll drive from the office
to the airport, which is however long.

00:27:36.620 --> 00:27:39.140
And he said if I've got the radio,

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:43.581
I'm afraid that I'll switch it on and
I won't be thinking about Microsoft.

00:27:43.581 --> 00:27:46.492
That's obsession.

00:27:46.492 --> 00:27:52.175
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I believe you.

00:27:52.175 --> 00:27:57.076
[LAUGH] So the funding environment
right now, as you alluded to,

00:27:57.076 --> 00:28:00.150
is becoming extremely competitive.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:03.020
There are a record number of firms-
&gt;&gt; I didn't allude to it.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:04.015
You alluded to it.

00:28:04.015 --> 00:28:05.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; True.

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:10.060
There are a record number of new venture
firms and capital in the market.

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:15.640
Just raised a $1 billion vision fund,
Sequoia just raised an $8 billion fund.

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:19.310
Are there too many investment
dollars chasing too few investable

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:20.180
opportunities right now?

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:25.908
&gt;&gt; So that's the line I've

00:28:25.908 --> 00:28:30.388
heard since 1986.

00:28:30.388 --> 00:28:36.197
With one or two exceptions,
maybe 1991, 1992.

00:28:36.197 --> 00:28:40.433
And then, right after the dot-com blow up,
when capital was,

00:28:40.433 --> 00:28:43.950
everybody understood
that capital was scarce.

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:46.198
It's always been a competitive
funding environment.

00:28:46.198 --> 00:28:52.490
There have always been, look,
how many companies get financed?

00:28:52.490 --> 00:28:57.330
And then how many become worth it,
become tremendously important companies?

00:28:57.330 --> 00:29:01.362
So if you just do that winnowing,
and look at the funnel,

00:29:01.362 --> 00:29:05.240
there are very, very few great companies.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:11.590
So everybody is madly in
pursuit of those companies,

00:29:11.590 --> 00:29:16.590
and obviously there are these tangible
examples of the enormous value that's

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:20.630
being created in the world of
technology in the last 30 or 40 years.

00:29:20.630 --> 00:29:23.760
And so, money has poured into the sector.

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:28.860
But for us, it's always been this sort of,
almost always been this sort of

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:34.790
environment where a really
spectacular idea can raise capital,

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:38.890
where somebody who's starting a company
usually has choices of capital.

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:42.150
I mean, that's the world that I grew
up in, that's the world that we've

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:46.070
always operated in, and I suspect will
be the world that we always operate in.

00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:48.530
It's just part of life in our business.

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:51.040
&gt;&gt; So no bubble.

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:52.972
You're good?

00:29:52.972 --> 00:29:56.985
&gt;&gt; Well, there are always
things that we will have done,

00:29:56.985 --> 00:30:01.697
others will have done that five,
ten years from now we'll say to

00:30:01.697 --> 00:30:05.814
ourselves how did we ever do
something as silly as that?

00:30:05.814 --> 00:30:08.470
Or wasn't it inane or crazy?

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:10.675
Or we didn't anticipate this, that, or

00:30:10.675 --> 00:30:11.874
the other
&gt;&gt; So

00:30:11.874 --> 00:30:14.961
I'm sure we'll have egg on
our face from time to time.

00:30:14.961 --> 00:30:17.870
Again, it's just an occupational hazard.

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:22.362
&gt;&gt; So I want to talk a little bit
about how Silicon Valley has changed.

00:30:22.362 --> 00:30:26.722
There continues to be many discussions
happening about how to make both

00:30:26.722 --> 00:30:30.284
technology companies and
venture capital more diverse,

00:30:30.284 --> 00:30:33.510
potentially beyond history majors.

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:38.263
What responsibility does the venture
industry have to fostering diversity in

00:30:38.263 --> 00:30:39.997
the technology ecosystem?

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:49.056
&gt;&gt; The responsibility
of the valley of firms

00:30:49.056 --> 00:30:53.943
like ours is to identify
burgeoning talent.

00:30:53.943 --> 00:30:59.653
And the more the merrier,
irrespective of gender,

00:30:59.653 --> 00:31:05.779
irrespective of background,
irrespective of color.

00:31:05.779 --> 00:31:11.850
Now, that's easy to say,
and it's a bland statement.

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:16.695
And you will then rightly say well,
what are you doing about it, and

00:31:16.695 --> 00:31:19.440
what are you doing about it actively?

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:26.050
So we're doing what we can, but
there's only so much that we can do.

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:30.420
Firms like ours are representative
of social trends.

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:33.430
And I sort of sensed that this
question might come up, so

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:38.518
I turned the question around because I
dug up the data on diversity at Stanford.

00:31:38.518 --> 00:31:42.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In majors.

00:31:42.430 --> 00:31:43.150
And it's shocking.

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:49.420
So let me give you some statistics.

00:31:49.420 --> 00:31:53.330
Because I think this whole issue is one

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:58.690
that all of us have something to do about.

00:31:58.690 --> 00:32:03.540
So and I'll give you one little
example say from the math

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.210
department in
the undergraduate program here.

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:11.079
And this is germane, I have a friend
of mine whose daughter is in the math

00:32:11.079 --> 00:32:16.597
department at the moment, and is thinking
about leaving the math department and

00:32:16.597 --> 00:32:20.840
switching to another major
because she is surrounded by men.

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:27.060
And so in 1983,
if you were an undergraduate at Stanford,

00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:33.320
there was only 22% of the students who had
elected math as their major were women.

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:40.269
Guess what that number was in 2014, which
is the last year when data was available.

00:32:40.269 --> 00:32:45.160
22% in 1983,

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:50.056
16% in 2014.

00:32:50.056 --> 00:32:55.799
If you're a computer science
graduate at Stanford,

00:32:55.799 --> 00:33:02.317
the percentage of women in
the class in 1983 was 21%.

00:33:02.317 --> 00:33:08.380
In 2014, 20%, and
the only point I'm making is,

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:14.340
we'll hire as quickly and
as merrily as we possibly can.

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:18.770
We have seven female
investment partners today,

00:33:18.770 --> 00:33:25.319
we've got I think close to half a dozen
female CEO's that we've backed.

00:33:25.319 --> 00:33:28.013
But we're a reflection of society and

00:33:28.013 --> 00:33:32.770
we can only change as the fast as
the rest of society will change.

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:34.790
But we're more than happy to do our part.

00:33:34.790 --> 00:33:41.050
We funded all these different
initiatives to help women in particular.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:47.170
In technology we have one of our partners,

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:50.240
is working with something
called female founders.

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:54.730
But it's imperative for
the high schools and for the universities

00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:59.680
of America to change before we'll see
the real acceleration in technology,

00:33:59.680 --> 00:34:05.910
which I gotta say is a lot better
today than it was 10 or 15 years ago.

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:08.400
And I think if you look at
the computer science numbers,

00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:13.130
those are the best numbers
that I could find at Stanford.

00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:15.520
But you even saw they're not overwhelming.

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:20.343
In 1983, the computer science
proportion of the computer

00:34:20.343 --> 00:34:23.619
science class here undergrad was 18%.

00:34:23.619 --> 00:34:28.385
It progressed but
in 2014 it was still only 28%.

00:34:28.385 --> 00:34:31.780
So there's tons of work for
all of us to do.

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:32.550
That's my only point.

00:34:33.990 --> 00:34:36.690
&gt;&gt; So I want to take our
discussion beyond the Valley

00:34:36.690 --> 00:34:41.830
because investing outside of Silicon
Valley is become a very popular topic for

00:34:41.830 --> 00:34:44.190
a lot of people including Sequoia.

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:46.590
Do you think this is still the best
place in the world to start a company?

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:53.750
&gt;&gt; It's here and it's in China.

00:34:54.980 --> 00:35:00.130
And their trials and
tribulations involved with both places.

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:04.490
And obviously everybody knows
about how expensive it is and

00:35:04.490 --> 00:35:08.800
the cost of living and
gross receipts taxing San Francisco and

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:12.110
all these other impediments
that we wish weren't here.

00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:13.410
But my goodness, gracious me.

00:35:14.550 --> 00:35:17.170
Much as people here will complain and

00:35:17.170 --> 00:35:19.920
moan about different aspects
of Silicone Valley, you go

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:24.570
to places elsewhere around the world,
they all wish they were Silicone Valley.

00:35:24.570 --> 00:35:30.422
So it's a spectacular, fantastic place
in which to start a company and then

00:35:33.295 --> 00:35:37.181
I was saying to your illustrious Dean,
before I came,

00:35:37.181 --> 00:35:42.333
while we were chatting backstage,
that I've spent the last three weeks

00:35:42.333 --> 00:35:47.680
travelling in China and Southeast Asia,
those are vibrant places.

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:52.166
One of the very big changes, China
obviously, everybody's read about but

00:35:52.166 --> 00:35:59.370
Southeast Asia, is really pulsing
with entrepreneurial activity,

00:35:59.370 --> 00:36:03.730
and that's a big change in the last five,
six years.

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:06.625
Everybody knows about India,
and talked about India, but

00:36:06.625 --> 00:36:12.010
Southeast Asia is surprisingly vibrant.

00:36:12.010 --> 00:36:17.260
So there are lots of the big change from
when I started in the venture business,

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:21.960
a long time ago now,
largely Silicon Valley, and

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:24.990
to some extent, Boston,
we had 100% market share give or

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:29.150
take on the most important
technology companies of tomorrow.

00:36:29.150 --> 00:36:30.880
That's completely changed.

00:36:30.880 --> 00:36:36.610
So that so much of the investment now
occurs outside of the United States for

00:36:36.610 --> 00:36:37.649
a whole variety of reasons.

00:36:39.348 --> 00:36:43.680
&gt;&gt; So I want to get a little bit of advice
before we turn it over to the audience.

00:36:43.680 --> 00:36:47.350
So I think we're all trying to determine
how to focus our time, energy, and

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:50.200
resources upon graduation.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:54.561
If you were to put yourself in
our shoes today, what problems or

00:36:54.561 --> 00:36:59.994
industries would you be focusing on maybe
another way to ask that question is,

00:36:59.994 --> 00:37:05.126
where is Sequoia going to be investing so
we can the bugs going to be.

